A password-confirmation form needs to compare two sibling inputs — a case where local state per field can't express the validation:

```jsx
function SignupForm() {
  const [password, setPassword] = useState("");
  const [confirm, setConfirm] = useState("");
  const mismatch = confirm.length > 0 && password !== confirm;

  return (
    <>
      <input value={password} onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)} />
      <input value={confirm} onChange={(e) => setConfirm(e.target.value)} />
      {mismatch && <p>Passwords don't match</p>}
    </>
  );
}
```

Neither `<input>` holds its own state — both are controlled by `SignupForm`, which is the only component that can see both values at once and compute `mismatch`. Note that `mismatch` itself isn't stored in state at all: it's computed fresh on every render from the two fields it depends on, which is the subject of the next slide.

```jsx
// Anti-pattern: syncing a derived value with an effect
const [fullName, setFullName] = useState("");
useEffect(() => {
  setFullName(firstName + " " + lastName);
}, [firstName, lastName]);
```

This *looks* reasonable — it even works most of the time — but it's strictly worse than deriving inline. It costs an **extra render** (state update → re-render → effect fires → another state update → another re-render), and it creates a window where `fullName` is briefly stale between the two renders. If a third value is later added to the dependency array and someone forgets to include it, `fullName` silently stops updating and nothing errors.

This is one of the most common causes of "my UI doesn't update" bug reports, and the fix is always the same: delete the effect and the extra state, and compute `fullName` directly in the render body. Effects exist for **synchronizing with something outside React** (a subscription, the DOM, an API call) — not for keeping two pieces of your own state in agreement, which derivation does for free.
